#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565534} :activity/id #uuid "00577aba-9963-5deb-b814-1ea4065846a2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6504e2ed-8c9d-555d-8fcf-97583995dc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565094} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00fba0-2dea-5ba8-b58e-77f73a4f66ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565122} {:answer/id #uuid "81118184-833a-5a24-a8a8-080246689d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565137} {:answer/id #uuid "b072fa82-4f42-520f-9a28-4cf9d7117181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565201} {:answer/id #uuid "dabc169e-2bef-5859-8318-71f376774cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565094} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27cb884-58cc-5969-9740-465e694a1153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b04f6fb-738e-5fda-ac4b-d00fe6581051" :question/math-academy-id "q-94303" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +1=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =12>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "19ff9675-2577-5ff5-a7b4-7a7e7b5cf512"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d288b96-5588-5a61-8707-1cd7320722ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351675} {:answer/id #uuid "320557e4-68a2-5c84-980d-3b3ee616da1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352030} {:answer/id #uuid "37b6a32d-8f94-5003-8bc3-b248e652ca30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352083} {:answer/id #uuid "53daa546-f7c8-5488-9b6d-80860ccb8bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352314} {:answer/id #uuid "f8491c4f-7494-58c4-8f86-8a6e40cbd288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351675} :answer-field/id #uuid "835f08c2-4c07-5aef-87e1-fbe4a54164d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ad91747-58ce-5cb7-bde7-896fa9da6f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-84607" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5e9e6af9-ebf7-5415-a0ec-ae18a3d81a55" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25571277-f7a5-5289-a8ba-fe98639976b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,2,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564996} {:answer/id #uuid "5224e1dd-be47-5d3a-96e7-1006ac12e8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565057} {:answer/id #uuid "57b32dc3-3595-5c57-8a49-20c39bedb11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565073} {:answer/id #uuid "ba07e6b8-5e9f-5a76-add8-0070f7af1151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565219} {:answer/id #uuid "d5124caf-6b29-53bb-9a3b-83f8084a1569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565073} :answer-field/id #uuid "c93dfc36-0f20-5d6b-ba00-c7c282eff3ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a321d767-b173-540f-8a02-df4dc515c060" :question/math-academy-id "q-127094" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2), & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=0\\,⟹\\,\\pi x=0+k\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=k,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only four lie within the interval $[0,3].$ These are $x=0,1,2,3,$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well, but they are included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1,2,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1))+2)=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (2))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (3))+2)=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "9b8993f0-411f-5a90-8d98-e97c774a5798" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7570ab-fd63-531d-8e02-29bf746ab1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559280} {:answer/id #uuid "933e3d56-7dd8-5f4b-8baf-cfed5b9e1537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559382} {:answer/id #uuid "9580e7d9-2197-512f-98b3-2771aef78be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559388} {:answer/id #uuid "9baad326-2a3f-5a1a-891d-cfc23346b6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559411} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcc144-7c34-56a2-8495-45807a3fe82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559496} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd1c54a-c831-5cbe-8eef-ef06db6fc42c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ade0c9c-871d-5f78-8adc-bcd701d0727a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106750" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second  equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2(-4) & =-10 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-4)=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c28639f1-c139-5eb1-b499-5035a6216a8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b969c54c-bfd6-5745-87c4-05539c2d858d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159711} {:answer/id #uuid "34019b57-f57b-5b58-ae30-9c2fbf9dcf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159713} {:answer/id #uuid "348fb3b8-e710-5dbe-80e3-014de77c13af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159714} {:answer/id #uuid "3e75a3b1-6dd8-5049-bd03-0ad759293c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565028} {:answer/id #uuid "74d609b3-8497-5d75-9db4-4e0b45bf9758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{y}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159711} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f37bb7-7f21-51c2-b7ae-c6c13364b14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2641b70-6005-5dd7-a26d-e0feed529994" :question/math-academy-id "q-145888" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>4,y>3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>4$ and $y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point. \n\n![](images/57/575107f600fc73bfa39978f161d9ba9da2ec9315d7be4487e7069ceb4ac590c6.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $4$ and $x>4,$ every value in the support of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $3$ and $y>3,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb0d7a20-49d9-5f92-88a2-05da28c97dd2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435776} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfe18bb-7b8e-5e23-b6bb-c3d39e3896af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435782} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dbcdf0-68f4-5a84-9587-f3fa35d6aba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}=-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435790} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdecbfa-f590-53c6-baa7-254b9858e4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435795} {:answer/id #uuid "f87318a8-3643-5bd8-8b57-84f2fc51ba95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}>-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435776} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b045b8-bf15-51b1-816f-a65dbb6c4b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722" :question/math-academy-id "q-158552" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc01329d-ea2a-5f16-9365-e0876b3fd961" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14be5dd6-fd36-5a08-a677-f6f5e4980f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564971} {:answer/id #uuid "5e88b7b2-c4a2-5f46-b0d0-299f26aaf066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565082} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ded33d-31c7-56d3-a266-03c82ae475fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565217} {:answer/id #uuid "de2afa21-f6ff-5915-a8ab-239729b5c4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565269} {:answer/id #uuid "e72807a2-0780-5981-82b2-b6a4a2adee11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564971} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1973d3-4cfd-50f8-81d5-f1c3b35c4846" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fe770e3a-7d81-54be-9346-77bf6627bbf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109835" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +e)^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},$ the nullity of $(D+eI)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(D+eI)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D+eI)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D+eI)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e.$"} :step/id #uuid "d376774b-1290-557f-84a3-c6190f1e317c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565532}}] :activity/title "Quiz 6" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564925}]}
